**The finding.** Kill criterion not met. The learned boundary is "always on" and agrees with the hand threshold on 41% of instances. In nodes, forcing Theorem 2 on where the hand rule turns it off saves on 8,810 instances, ties on 21,109 and costs on one, and all fifteen dense 125×125 refutations save. But the fixed rule costs 1.17× per node, so on the clock it is slower on 347 of 350 heavy hand-off instances, and the seconds sweep is flat for thresholds between 4.5 and 6. The proposed rule, stated and not applied, is to keep `sparse_enough_for_better_move` at 5 when seconds are the budget, and the lever for changing that is the per-node cost of the C `better_move` pass, not the threshold.

